<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 4 – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> I Can Connect with Nature!</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> I'm Outside. I'm Happy!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b><br>
            Writing: Productive: Develop simple responses to questions based on reading texts.<br>
            Interactive: Participate in group-based brainstorming to draft ideas for a text.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b><br>
            Writing: Can develop simple responses to questions about nature from reading texts (e.g., "I see a tree.").<br>
            Can participate in group-based brainstorming to draft and organize ideas for a nature text, using drawings and labels if needed (e.g., "The sun is bright.'').<br>
            Can write simple sentences to describe abilities and observations related to nature using familiar vocabulary. (e.g., "I can see the sun." "I can't hear the wind.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; background-color: #e0e0e0;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; background-color: #e0e0e0;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; background-color: #e0e0e0;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a large picture of a beautiful nature scene (e.g., a park with trees, flowers, a stream, birds, sun). Students look at the picture. The teacher asks, "What do you see?" and guides them to write 1-2 words on small whiteboards or scratch paper. For example, "tree," "flower," "bird." This activates vocabulary and prepares for writing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher points to a "tree" in the picture and says, "I see a tree. I can see a green tree." The teacher writes "I see a tree." on the board. The teacher then points to the sun and says, "I can see the sun." The teacher clearly articulates the /k/ sound in "can" and the /s/ sound in "sun," emphasizing the blending of /s/ /ʌ/ /n/ to form "sun." The teacher models how to form simple sentences for observations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you see the sun?" (Students nod or say "Yes, I can.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is the bird under the tree or on the tree?" (Students point or say "On the tree.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 8-10 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, simple descriptive paragraph (2-3 sentences) titled "My Day in the Park" about a child's experience connecting with nature, displayed on a projector or large print. The text uses familiar vocabulary (park, tree, flowers, birds, sun, grass, beautiful, hear, see, can, relax) and simple present tense with prepositions of place. Example: "I am in the park. It is a beautiful day. I can see green trees and colorful flowers. I hear birds singing. The sun is bright in the sky. I can relax on the soft grass."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Expressing connection with nature by talking about sensory observations and experiences in nature (e.g., "I hear the birds." "The flowers smell nice.") and locating people and things (e.g., "I am near the big tree.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students read the text silently first, then the teacher reads it aloud, highlighting the pronunciation of /k/ in "can" and /s/ in "sun" and blending of "flower."</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "Where is the child?" (Students write "In the park" on their whiteboards.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching (writing):</b> The teacher provides two simple questions based on the text for students to write short answers:
                        <ul style="margin-top: 4px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What can the child see? (e.g., "Trees and flowers.")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What can the child hear? (e.g., "Birds.")</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 10-12 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Writing)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice writing simple sentences using 'Can/Can't', prepositions of place (in, on, near), and present simple, incorporating vocabulary: tree, grass, flower, bird, sun, sky, listen, see, relax, beautiful, green, bright.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1 (Controlled Practice - Cloze & Prepositions):</b> The teacher distributes a worksheet with simple sentences about a nature scene, leaving blanks for 'can/can't' and prepositions of place. Students complete the sentences.
                        <ul style="margin-top: 4px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I ____ see a butterfly." (can)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The bird is ____ the tree." (on/in/near)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I ____ hear the wind." (can't)</li>
                        </ul>
                        Teacher circulates, offering help with spelling and grammar.
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2 (Semi-controlled - Sentence Building):</b> Students are given a list of words/phrases (e.g., "I / can / see / a / beautiful / flower," "The / sun / is / bright," "A / spider / is / on / the / leaf.") and sentence frames (e.g., "I can see a ____ ____." "The ____ is ____.") They cut and paste or rewrite to form complete, grammatically correct sentences on their worksheets. The teacher explicitly checks for the correct use of "can/can't" and present simple.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3 (Interactive Brainstorming for Writing):</b> Students work in small groups (3-4 students). The teacher shows a new, different picture of a nature spot (e.g., a river with a waterfall, a trail). Each group receives a large sheet of paper. They brainstorm ideas for writing about the picture. They can draw simple elements and label them, or write short phrases about what they *see*, *hear*, or *feel*. Examples: "tree (green)", "water fall (loud)", "I can walk", "The air is peaceful". This directly targets the objective "Participate in group-based brainstorming to draft ideas for a text." Teacher encourages polite requests: "Can you draw a flower, please?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 15-18 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> Individual written product based on group brainstorming and a new visual prompt.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b> The teacher displays a third, new picture of a nature scene (e.g., a quiet forest trail). Students take their brainstorming sheets from Activity 3 (or the teacher provides a blank worksheet with prompts). Individually, they write 3-5 simple sentences describing what they see, hear, or can do in this new nature scene, using their brainstormed vocabulary and the grammar from the lesson (Can/Can't, prepositions, present simple). They are encouraged to describe abilities and observations, such as "I can see a big tree." "I can't hear cars here." "The air is fresh." "I am on a trail." This addresses the learning outcome "Can write simple sentences to describe abilities and observations related to nature." and "Can develop simple responses to questions about nature."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme/Scenario:</b> The writing task allows students to express their connection to nature by describing their observations and feelings, aligning with "I Can Connect with Nature!" and "I'm Outside. I'm Happy!" They can write sentences like "I like this quiet trail." or "It's peaceful here."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 8-10 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment and self-reflection)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist and peer assessment.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students exchange their written sentences from Stage 4 with a peer. They use a simple checklist to review their partner's writing. The checklist includes:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Are there 3-5 sentences? (Yes/No)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Are "can" or "can't" used? (Yes/No)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Are words about nature used (tree, flower, bird, sun)? (Yes/No)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of simple sentences (are they easy to understand?).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target vocabulary (e.g., tree, grass, bird, beautiful, quiet).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of 'can/can't' and prepositions of place (e.g., "I can see a bird. It is on the tree.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Participation in group-based brainstorming.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer on a small piece of paper:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can write about ____ in nature." (e.g., "what I see," "what I hear")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new word I used today is ____."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 5-7 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Student and teacher reflection)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was easy to write about nature today?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was difficult when writing sentences?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this writing lesson? (e.g., student engagement in brainstorming, clear sentences).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for the next lesson (Lesson 5: Mediation & Integration), especially for students struggling with sentence structure or vocabulary recall? (e.g., more sentence frames, small group remediation). I need to ensure the vocabulary and simple sentence structures are solid for the final project.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This writing practice will prepare students for Lesson 5, where they will use their accumulated vocabulary and sentence structures to mediate information and create a short text as part of their 21st Century Skills Project, summarizing their experiences and observations in nature.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 4-5 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>